        {
          "title": "3. Power and Paying the Bill",
          "sequenceOrder": 3,
          "status": "published",
          "xpReward": 55,
          "maxReattempts": 0,
          "passingThreshold": 60,
          "estimatedDuration": 10,
          "difficulty": "HARD",
          "learnBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "p911-3-1",
              "type": "blob_dialog",
              "content": "Your electricity bill is not measured in volts or amps. It is measured in energy.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "lines": [
                  {
                    "id": "l1",
                    "text": "Your electricity bill is not measured in volts or amps. It is measured in energy.",
                    "mood": "thinking",
                    "cta": "Which is?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l2",
                    "text": "Power times time. Power itself is voltage times current.",
                    "mood": "happy",
                    "cta": "Show me"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l3",
                    "text": "A 1000 W iron for one hour uses one kilowatt hour. That is one unit on the bill.",
                    "mood": "happy",
                    "cta": "That is all?"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p911-3-2",
              "type": "formula",
              "content": "P = VI = I^2R \\qquad E = Pt"
            },
            {
              "id": "p911-3-3",
              "type": "ohms_law_lab",
              "content": "Watch the current climb as you raise the voltage. Power climbs faster, because it depends on both.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "voltage": 9,
                "resistance": 3,
                "maxVoltage": 12,
                "maxResistance": 12,
                "caption": "At 9 V through 3 ohm the current is 3 A, so the power is 27 W."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p911-3-4",
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Because **P = I squared R**, doubling the current gives four times the heating. That is why a thin overloaded wire fails so suddenly."
            }
          ],
          "evaluateBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "p911-eb-3-1",
              "type": "tap_target",
              "question": "A heater draws 5 A from a 230 V supply. What is its power?",
              "correctAnswer": "elevenfifty",
              "explanation": "P = VI = 230 x 5 = 1150 W.",
              "metadata": {
